  0.0260973}}]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A  general proof", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Just to show ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is up to it, we can run things for  ",
  StyleBox["arbitrary",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points \[LongDash] without trying to simplify the algebra. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["Here are three quite general points:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(u\_1 = aa\_1\ u + bb\_1\ v + cc\_1\ w; \n
    v\_1 = aa\_2\ u + bb\_2\ v + cc\_2\ w; \n
    w\_1 = aa\_3\ u + bb\_3\ v + cc\_3\ w; \)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is the hexagon:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(A\_1 = a\_1\ u\_1 + b\_1\ w\_1; \nA\_2 = a\_2\ u\_1 + b\_2\ w\_1; \n
    A\_3 = a\_3\ u\_1 + b\_3\ w\_1; \nB\_1 = c\_1\ v\_1 + d\_1\ w\_1; \n
    B\_2 = c\_2\ v\_1 + d\_2\ w\_1; \nB\_3 = c\_3\ v\_1 + d\_3\ w\_1; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["We form the three Pappus points:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(C\_1 = PE[PE[A\_2, B\_3], PE[A\_3, B\_2]] // Tidy; \n
    C\_2 = PE[PE[A\_1, B\_3], PE[A\_3, B\_1]] // Tidy; \n
    C\_3 = PE[PE[A\_1, B\_2], PE[A\_2, B\_1]] // Tidy; \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["Now take a look.  You can see the problem with the algebra.", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(C\_1\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["We try to get the pappus line.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(pappus = PE[C\_1, C\_2] // Tidy\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["It works.  We can try to factor.", "Text"],

Cell["pappus = Tidy/@Factor[pappus]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It works.  And that looks reasonable, but we will not try to cancel.  So, \
let us see whether the point ",
  StyleBox["C3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " lies on this line."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  PageBreakAbove->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[PD[pappus, C\_3]]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["It does!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  So that proves the theorem as a general statement.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData["Menelaus\[CloseCurlyQuote] Theorem"], "Subsection",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The problem", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If a line cuts the sides of a triangle,  then there is an ",
  StyleBox["interesting algebraic relationship",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " between the numbers that represent the relative coordinates of the points \
of intersection on each of the sides.  "
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Problem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "How can we ",
  StyleBox["discover",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  the relationship?"
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The symbolic representation", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In order to express this in our notation, we take as the given triangle \
the",
  StyleBox[" standard triangle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with vertices ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  We then position three points arbitrarily on the three sides using ",
  StyleBox["relative homogeneous coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on each of the lines.  First, we make sure the variables have no values."
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[Subscript]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(P\_1 = a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v; \nP\_2 = a\_2\ v + b\_2\ w; \n
    P\_3 = a\_3\ w + b\_3\ u; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, the three points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are arbitrary and are not in general going to be ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Recall, however, that we know an ",
  StyleBox["algebraic test",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for collinerarity:"
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[PE[P\_1, P\_2], P\_3] == 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So this is the conclusion:",
  StyleBox[" the points are collinear ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["if and only if",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["  this algebraic relationship holds.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is an useful exercise to interpret this theorem in special cases.  In \
the Cartesian plane we might think of the triangle as being made of the ",
  StyleBox["origin",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and the two ",
  StyleBox["unit points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the axes.  We then parameterize the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x + y == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Here is the notation for this set-up:"
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c]; \nQ\_1 = a\ u + w; \nQ\_2 = b\ v + w; \n
    Q\_3 = \((1 - c)\)\ u + c\ v + w; \n
    ExpandAll[PD[PE[Q\_1, Q\_2], Q\_3] == 0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is the algebraic relationship.  It might be more helpful to solve for \
the value of ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput"],
  " that makes the equation true."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Solve[%,{c}]// Simplify", "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some Constructions", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Compositions of perspectivities", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A perspective transformation", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "A we have discussed before, a ",
  StyleBox["perspective projection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the points of one line onto the points of another is obtained by using \
a ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for the projection.  We are going to implement such a transformation by \
using ",
  StyleBox["PE", "InlineInput"],
  " to transcribe the obvious construction into algebra.  Here is a picture \
of such a transformation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(CC = \(-5\)\ u + 9\ v + w; \nll = 2  x - y + z; \n\t
    Draw[Join[{y, ll}, \n\t\tTable[PE[CC, a\ u + w], {a, \(-8\), 13}]]]; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It can be seen that we have taken ",
  StyleBox["equally spaced",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "points along the horizontal line and projected them to ",
  StyleBox["non-equally spaces",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points along the sloped line.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "One represention of (finite) points on the horizontal is notating points \
there as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let us see how they are transformed."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PP = PE[ll, PE[CC, a\ u + w]] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can find the Cartesian coordinates by dividing by the coefficient of ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput"],
  ".  In terms of such coordinates, then, we could think of the \
transformation as being \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({a, 0} 
        \[Rule] {\(\(-5\) + 8\ a\)\/\(19 + 2  a\), 
          \(9 + 18  a\)\/\(19 + 2  a\)}\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", \nwhich fails to produce a point on the sloped line when the parameter ",
  
  StyleBox["a", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " equals ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(-\(19\/2\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  (Why?)"
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Using two central perspectivities", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be two arbitrary points in the plane, and let ",
  StyleBox["ll",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be an arbitrary line."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(A\_1 = a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v + c\_1\ w; \n
    A\_2 = a\_2\ u + b\_2\ v + c\_2\ w; \nll = a\_3\ x + b\_3\ y + c\_3\ z; 
    \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We are going to use the points as centers to project the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis onto ",
  StyleBox["ll",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and then to project ",
  StyleBox["ll",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " onto the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  In this way we get a ",
  StyleBox["composition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of two perspective transformations.  Here we go:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(X = d\ u + e\ w; \)\), 
    \(Y = PE[PE[PE[PE[X, A\_1], ll], A\_2], x] // Tidy\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This looks better if we write the coefficients in terms of ",
  StyleBox["d",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["e",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Y = Map[Collect[#,{d,e}]&, Y, {2}]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In other words, we see that the ",
  StyleBox["homogeneous",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " coordinates ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({d, e}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " for the point on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis are transformed into ",
  StyleBox["linear combinations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of these quantities as coordinates along the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "This will be eventually seen as a special case of a general fact: ",
  StyleBox[
  "projective transformations are linear transformation of coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Adjusting the centers", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Suppose we have distinct points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["X",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis, and we want to map them to distinct points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Y",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " over on the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  ",
  StyleBox["Can we do this by a projective transformation?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The answer is yes.  We work out a special case when the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are neither at infinity nor at the origin.   We make a perspective \
projection onto a third line and then to the final one."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The secret is to let the third line be the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(ll == PE[A\_1, B\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We project from the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis onto it and then onto the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  (Draw a figure!)  If the center ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and the center ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then it will be the case that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is transformed into ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  is transformed into ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Draw a figure!!!)  The ",
  StyleBox["trick",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is to adjust one of the centers until ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " gets transformed into ",
  StyleBox["Y", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It turns out that the center ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "can be chosen as ",
  StyleBox["fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and only the other center has to be adjusted.  Here is the set-up we can \
use ",
  StyleBox["before",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " we know where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " should be."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[Subscript]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(A\_1 = w + a\_1\ u; \nA\_2 = w + a\_2\ u; \nB\_1 = w + b\_1\ v; \n
    B\_2 = w + b\_2\ v; \nC\_1 = c\_1\ A\_1 + c\_2\ B\_1; \n
    C\_2 = A\_2 + B\_2; \nll = PE[A\_1, B\_2]; \nX = d\_1\ w + e\_1\ u; \n
    Y = d\_2\ w + e\_2\ v; \)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Here is the transform that results as a composition of two \
perspectivities.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(trans[p_] := PE[PE[PE[PE[p, C\_2], ll], C\_1], x]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, whatever ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "is, we can show that the two special points get transformed to the right \
places."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[B\_1, trans[A\_1]] // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[B\_2, trans[A\_2]] // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For the point ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput"],
  ", however, we have to do some work."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(YY = trans[X] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell["This would be better factored.", "Text"],

Cell["YY = Factor[YY]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The cases where ",
  StyleBox["a1 == 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["b2 == 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are not being allowed, so we may divide out these factors."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(YY = \(-\(YY\/\(a\_1\ b\_2\)\)\) // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell["We can reformat the multipliers.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(YY = Map[Collect[#1, {d\_1, e\_1}]&, YY, {2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point ",
  StyleBox["YY", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is where ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " gets transformed with the center ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as shown above.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Can we choose the parameters determining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "so that ",
  StyleBox["Y == YY", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "?  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a try.  There are two parameters in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " which also turn up in ",
  StyleBox["YY", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and there are two coordinates for ",
  StyleBox["YY", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This gives us two (linear) equations in two unknowns. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = 
      Simplify[Solve[{Coefficient[YY, w, 1] == d\_2, 
            Coefficient[YY, v, 1] == e\_2}, {c\_1, c\_2}]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And if we remember that the points are distinct, the denominators will ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " vanish.  Thus, the solutions look good, so we check that the image point \
",
  StyleBox["YY", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " indeed comes out to be the desired point ",
  StyleBox["Y", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[YY /. sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["It works! ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " So the problem is ",
  StyleBox["solvable",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This is a special case of a general theorem about constructing \
projective transformations from central perspectivities.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Conclusion:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "For achieving a projective transformation between two distinct lines it \
seems that ",
  StyleBox["two perspectivities",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "are enough."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The discussion is not quite complete, however, because we only \
investigated the images of ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points.  When we learn about ",
  StyleBox["crossratios",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " we will see that indeed that is the only freedom we had in setting up a \
projective transformation between lines."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Defining the 4th harmonic", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The construction", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Think of a line.  It might as well be the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  And think of any three points, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", on this line.  One of them might as well be at the origin and one other \
at the unit point, but the third should be arbitrary.  Here they are:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, Subscript]; \n\nA\_1\  = \ w; \ B\_1\  = \ u\  + \ w; 
    \ C\_1\  = \ a\ u\  + \ b\ w; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, take a point \"anywhere\" in the plane not  on the line ",
  StyleBox["y", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Such a choice can be represented as follows:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(X = c\_1\ u + v + c\_2\ w; \)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Join ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " each to ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " forming two lines.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(l\_1 = PE[X, A\_1]; l\_2 = PE[X, B\_1]; \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Take a line passing through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[LongDash] but ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  passing through ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  equal to the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  Such a line can be notated as follows, where neither ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(d\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " nor ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(d\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(l\_3 = d\_1\ PE[C\_1, X] + d\_2\ y; \)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That line is clearly distinct from the two lines, but let us check it \
passes through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[l\_3, C\_1]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Good.  Now intersect the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with the other two lines and make a cross with lines through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(l\_4 = PE[A\_1, PE[l\_3, l\_2]]; \nl\_5 = PE[B\_1, PE[l\_3, l\_1]]; 
    \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point at the intersection of these two lines is very interesting \
\[LongDash] especially if we join it to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["X",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and then project it down to the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(l\_6 = PE[X, PE[l\_4, l\_5]]; \nD\_1 = PE[l\_6, y] // Tidy\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[
"The answer can be simplified by dividing out the common factor."], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(D\_1 = Tidy/@Factor[D\_1]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since the factor is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{" ", 
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"-", 
            StyleBox[\(d\_1\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
          StyleBox[" ",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[\(d\_2\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}]]],
  " \[LongDash] and since we assumed that neither of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(d\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(d\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is zero \[LongDash] it is fair to divide it out.  The answer now no \
longer depends on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(d\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(d\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which means it ",
  StyleBox["does not depend",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the choice of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The answer also ",
  StyleBox["does not depend",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the choice of ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " either, since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " do not occur in the formula.  Thus, by what we have proved, the \
construction gives a ",
  StyleBox["unique point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The point  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(D\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is called ",
  StyleBox["the 4th harmonic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with respect to the pair of points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we set ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " equal to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(D\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and go through the whole calculation again, here is what we get:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(C\_1 = D\_1\/\(d\_1\ d\_2\); \nl\_3 = d\_1\ PE[C\_1, X] + d\_2\ y; \n
    l\_4 = PE[A\_1, PE[l\_3, l\_2]]; \nl\_5 = PE[B\_1, PE[l\_3, l\_1]]; \n
    l\_6 = PE[X, PE[l\_4, l\_5]]; \nD\_1 = Tidy/@Factor[PE[l\_6, y]]\)], 
  "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is the ",
  StyleBox["original point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " back again (after division).  ",
  StyleBox["The realtionship is thus entirely reciprocal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " We say that the pair of points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["divides",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(D\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["harmonically",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["This relationship has many symmetries, as we shall see.", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Making illustrations", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[
"Before drawing a picture to illustrate the construction of the 4th harmonic, \
we run the code that restores the original meaning of the points and lines in \
the construction."], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(C\_1 = a\ u + b\ w; \nl\_3 = d\_1\ PE[C\_1, X] + d\_2\ y; \n
    l\_4 = PE[A\_1, PE[l\_3, l\_2]]; \nl\_5 = PE[B\_1, PE[l\_3, l\_1]]; \n
    l\_6 = PE[X, PE[l\_4, l\_5]]; \nD\_1 = Tidy/@Factor[PE[l\_6, y]]; \n
    D\_1 = Tidy[\(-D\_1\)/\((\(d\_1\) d\_2)\)]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["\<\
Next, by giving values to the parameters, we can look at a special \
case.  \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 5; \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ b = 1; \nc\_1 = 1\/4; \ \ \ \ c\_2 = 1\/4; \n
    d\_1 = \(-\(1\/3\)\); \ d\_2 = 1; \n
    harm\_1 = 
      Draw[{y, \n
          \t\t{A\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(A\_1\)\>\""}, {B\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(B\_1\)\>\""}, 
          \n\t\t{C\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(C\_1\)\>\""}, {D\_1, 
            \*"\"\<\!\(D\_1\)\>\""}, {X, "\<X\>"}, \n\t\ \ \ \ l\_1, l\_2, 
          l\_3, l\_4, l\_5, l\_6}]; \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we can move ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput"],
  " around."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 5; b = 1; \nc\_1 = \(-\(1\/4\)\); c\_2 = \(-\(1\/5\)\); \n
    d\_1 = \(-\(1\/3\)\); d\_2 = \(-\(1\/2\)\); \n
    harm\_2 = 
      Draw[{y, \n
          \t\t{A\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(A\_1\)\>\""}, {B\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(B\_1\)\>\""}, 
          \n\t\t{C\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(C\_1\)\>\""}, {D\_1, 
            \*"\"\<\!\(D\_1\)\>\""}, {X, "\<X\>"}, \n\t\ \ \ \ l\_1, l\_2, 
          l\_3, l\_4, l\_5, l\_6}]; \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Looking at the two pictures together shows how the construction is \
independent of the choices.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[{harm\_1, harm\_2}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another interesting special case is when the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is moved off to infinity."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 5; \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ b = 0; \nc\_1 = 1\/4; \ \ \ \ c\_2 = 1\/4; \n
    d\_1 = \(-\(1\/3\)\); \ d\_2 = 1; \n
    Draw[{y, \n
        \t\t{A\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(A\_1\)\>\""}, {B\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(B\_1\)\>\""}, \n
        \t\t{C\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(C\_1\)\>\""}, {D\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(D\_1\)\>\""}, {
          X, "\<X\>"}, \n\t\ \ \ \ l\_1, l\_2, l\_3, l\_4, l\_5, l\_6}]; \)], 
  "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As we know already that the result is independent of ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we can move it off to infinity as well."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 5; \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ b = 0; \nc\_1 = 1\/4; \ \ \ \ c\_2 = 0; \n
    d\_1 = \(-\(1\/3\)\); \ d\_2 = 1; \n
    Draw[{y, \n
        \t\t{A\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(A\_1\)\>\""}, {B\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(B\_1\)\>\""}, \n
        \t\t{C\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(C\_1\)\>\""}, {D\_1, \*"\"\<\!\(D\_1\)\>\""}, {
          X, "\<X\>"}, \n\t\ \ \ \ l\_1, l\_2, l\_3, l\_4, l\_5, l\_6}]; \)], 
  "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["This picture illustrates the following:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The 4th harmonic of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with respect to the point at infinity on the line joining them is the ",
  StyleBox["midpoint",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  of the segment.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["We can also get this result by computation of coordinates:", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 1; b = 0; \nc\_1 = 1; c\_2 = 0; \nd\_1 = \(-\(1\/3\)\); d\_2 = 1; \n
    Expand[D\_1\/2]\)], "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Even symbolic coordinates for the construction give the same \
result.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 1; b = 0; \nc\_1 = 1; c\_2 = t; \nd\_1 = 1; d\_2 = s; \n
    Expand[D\_1\/2]\)], "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, Subscript]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A picture of many harmonies", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To ",
  StyleBox["draw",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the following picture we are making use of some facts about projective \
transformations we are only going to explain later.  But once we ",
  StyleBox["have",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the picture, we can describe what it ",
  StyleBox["means",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Draw[
      Join[{z}, \n\t\tTable[x - 2\ n\ z, {n, \(-2\), 2}], \n\t\t
        Table[y - 2  n\ z, {n, \(-2\), 2}], 
        Table[x - y - 2  n\ z, {n, \(-3\), 3}], 
        Table[x + y - 2  n\ z, {n, \(-3\), 3}]], 
      To \[Rule] {w, u + w, v + w, x + 2\ y - 30\ z}]; \)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "What this shows is that the drawings made for 4th harmonics can be ",
  StyleBox["iterated",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to certain kinds of ",
  StyleBox["nets",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of quadrilaterals.  So, if we decree that the slanted line is the \
\"horizon\", and that one of the quadrilaterals in the picture is a \
\"square\", then the rest of the picture is ",
  StyleBox["self-generating",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This then can be considered a ",
  StyleBox["perspective view",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  of a ",
  StyleBox["floor of square tiles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Perhaps as seen from an airplane while making a turn?)  We also note \
that the four \"vanishing points\" on the line at the horizon are are in the \
harmonic relationship.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We will justify by transformations later the assertion that ",
  StyleBox["any such",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " net really can be projected into a ",
  StyleBox["square net",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
